      "text": "microcrystals, and surface modified organoclays have been developed to achieve higher PFAA selectivity and capacity, and greater cost effec - tiveness [19 \u2013 21] . While several novel adsorbents have shown promising results in lab-scale tests (e.g., batch equilibrium experiments), there is a lack of testing in continuous flow configurations with real water matrices along with a shortage of parallel performance comparisons between novel adsorbents and commercially mature adsorbents [19] . Surface modified bentonite organoclay (SMC), a promising novel adsorbent that is commercially available, was selected for further investigation based on its availability at a competitive unit adsorbent price and high PFAA capacity as was demonstrated in previous studies [20,22,23] . The resource intensiveness (e.g., water demands, length of opera - tion) of column experiments has led to the application of rapid small- scale column tests (RSSCTs) as an alternative approach for a realistic assessment of multiple adsorbents [16,24 \u2013 27] . Recently, RSSCTs have been employed to evaluate PFAA removal by various adsorbents pri - marily from impaired groundwater resources [18,24,26] . Investigated RSSCT studies include assessing and comparing commercially estab - lished GAC and IX products for treatment of PFAAs contaminated groundwater [18] , validating the use of RSSCTs to evaluate IX for PFAA"
